==English==

===Etymology===
Compound of {{compound|red|neck|lang=en}}. Compare {{m|en|rooinek}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈɹɛdnɛk/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|slang|pejorative}} A poor, [[rural]], especially [[white]] [[Southern United States|Southern]] person (of either gender); especially, one who is regarded as [[unsophisticated]], [[rude]], [[racist]], and/or [[lowbrow]], etc.
# {{lb|en|slang}} The nickname given to miners who wore red [[bandana]]s for identification during the West Virginia mine war of 1921.<ref>[http://www.wvculture.org/hiStory/minewars.html West Virginia Division of Culture and History]</ref>
# {{lb|en|slang}} The nickname given to Baltimore street gang, active in 1859.<ref>[https://www.newspapers.com/clip/6830706/red_necks_are_violent_political_people/ The New York Times (New York, New York), 3 Nov 1859, Thu • Page 4 ]</ref>
# {{lb|en|UK|archaic}} The nickname given to [[Roman Catholics]] in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries.<ref>[https://www.newspapers.com/clip/6830356/redneck_a_term_used_by_protestants_in/ The Raleigh Register, (Raleigh, North Carolina),19 Mar 1841, Fri • Page 2 ]</ref>

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|bogan}} {{qualifier|Australia}}, {{l|en|cracker}}, {{l|en|hick}}, {{l|en|hillbilly}}, {{l|en|peckerwood}}, {{l|en|white trash}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|redneckish}}
* {{l|en|redneckism}}
* {{l|en|rednecky}}

